  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Shower/storm chances increase Thu-Fri\n\nAfter a mainly dry Tue-Wed, another front will work in from the\nwest. We are watching the western Gulf where potential tropical\nsystem may move toward the southeast ahead of this front to\nbring more moisture into the area. Models tend to lean toward\nthe heavier rainfall in the lower MS Valley/TN Valley and west\nof the Appalachians. \n\nIsolated to scattered severe thunderstorms will be possible\nThursday. Damaging wind gusts will be the main hazard with this\nactivity.\n\nVery strong west/southwesterly upper flow will reside north of\nour area Thursday. Nevertheless, intense deep-layer flow will \nstill support strong to severe wind gusts with convection \nthrough early evening. Richer boundary layer moisture will be in\nplace, fostering strong destabilization. Clusters and line \nsegments will pose a risk for mainly damaging gusts.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Dry weekend but more storm chances early next\nweek.\n\nHigh pressure follows the front Saturday into Sunday. Models\nagree on a broad trough over the Great Lakes into the mid-MS\nValley heading into Monday swinging through the mountains early\nnext week. This feature with the front and good moisture\nadvection should provide another opportunity for showers and\nstorms, but like previous systems, not everyone will get rain,\nbut those who do could get a decent downpour.",
